<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Stand Up!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Engage in basic interactive games with instructions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'sit down' with gestures).
Can follow visual signals to do tasks (e.g., 'clean up' supported with a picture).
Can engage in games by responding to simple questions (e.g., 'What is this?' 'School.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Good Morning, everyone!" and leads a simple "Hello Song" with actions, incorporating "stand up" and "sit down." The teacher says, "Please, stand up!" and "Please, sit down!" while modeling the actions and encouraging students to follow. This activates prior knowledge of classroom routines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher uses large, colorful flashcards and realia (e.g., a book, a child's chair, a teacher pointing to herself) to introduce core vocabulary: 'book', 'chair', 'teacher', 'student', 'window', 'door', 'desk'. For verbs, the teacher performs actions while saying the word: 'stand up', 'jump', 'listen', 'touch', 'open'. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /s/ in 'school' and /l/ in 'listen', asking students to repeat. Syllable stress in 'teacher' and 'student' is highlighted through clapping. Initial sounds /b/ 'book', /l/ 'listen', /t/ 'touch' are also emphasized.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new words and actions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a flashcard of a book) "Is this a book?" (Students nod or shake head)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher stands up) "Am I standing up?" (Students nod or shake head)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a chair) "What is this?" (Encourage single word answer or pointing to flashcard: "Chair.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio narrative with accompanying animated visuals or a sequence of large, illustrated flashcards depicting a school day routine. The narrative focuses on a child arriving at school and following simple instructions. Example: "Good morning, children! It's a school day. Please, listen. Please, sit on your chair. Open your book. It is a nice book. The teacher says, 'Stand up!' The student says, 'Yes!'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Following instructions: Students respond to commands like 'stand up' and 'listen'. Responding questions: Students answer basic WH-questions like 'What is it?' about school objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Visuals/Audio):</b> The teacher plays the audio or shows the picture sequence. "What day is it?" (Students point to 'school day' picture). "Are they happy?" (Students nod/shake head).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher replays the audio. When students hear a target word (e.g., 'book', 'chair', 'teacher', 'stand up'), they point to the corresponding object or flashcard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Gesture Response):</b> The teacher makes simple statements about the visuals. "The child is sitting." (Teacher shows picture of child standing). Students respond with thumbs up (True) or thumbs down (False).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Imperatives (e.g., 'Please stand up!', 'Please listen!', 'Touch the desk!'), Present Simple ('It is a book.'), WH-questions ('What is it?').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> 'chair', 'desk', 'table', 'book', 'teacher', 'student', 'window', 'door', 'listen', 'stand up', 'jump', 'touch', 'open'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Following instructions by responding to commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Recognizing polite instructions with "Please."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Teacher Says" (TPR Game):</b> The teacher leads a "Teacher Says" game. The teacher gives commands using target vocabulary and gestures (e.g., "Teacher says, 'Please, stand up!'", "Teacher says, 'Touch your desk!'", "Teacher says, 'Please, open your book!'", "Teacher says, 'Jump!'"). Students listen carefully and perform the action only when "Teacher says" is included. This reinforces following instructions and listening for specific cues.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Listen and Circle/Color":</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of various school objects (e.g., 'book', 'chair', 'desk', 'window', 'door') and children performing actions (e.g., 'standing up', 'sitting down', 'listening'). The teacher calls out instructions: "Please, circle the book!" "Please, color the student standing up!" Students listen and respond by marking the correct image. This focuses on auditory discrimination and comprehension with visual support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Listen and Check":</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives an instruction (e.g., "Please, touch your chair near the desk."). Both students perform the action. Then, the teacher asks, "Is your partner touching the chair?" Students listen to the question and respond non-verbally with a nod (yes) or shake (no), or by briefly pointing to their partner's correct action. This encourages active listening and observation in a low-pressure peer interaction context.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Interactive classroom game: "School Day Actions Challenge."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students demonstrate their ability to follow a sequence of school-related instructions, simulating actions they might perform during a school day. "Stand Up!" and "Listen!" are central to this activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short, calm piece of music. Students move freely around the classroom.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When the music stops, the teacher gives a two-step instruction related to a school day, e.g., "Please, stand up and open your book!" or "Please, touch the window and then sit on your chair!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen carefully to the compound instruction and perform both actions in the correct sequence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The game continues with different combinations of imperative verbs and school nouns (e.g., "Jump near the door!", "Listen and sit calm on your chair!", "Touch the top of your desk and then the bottom!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Emphasis is on listening for all parts of the instruction and performing them accurately, demonstrating fluency in following multiple commands.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student responses and an informal "What is this?" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Listen and Do" Quick Check:</b> The teacher gives 3-4 simple, distinct instructions (e.g., "Please, touch the door.", "Stand up, please.", "Open your book."). The teacher observes if students correctly follow each command.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"What is this?" Game:</b> The teacher points to 3-4 classroom objects (e.g., 'chair', 'desk', 'window', 'teacher') and asks, "What is this?" Students listen and respond by pointing to the correct flashcard or saying the single word (e.g., "Chair.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows simple instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'stand up', 'sit down').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (by pointing or single word) target vocabulary when asked 'What is this?'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to basic WH-questions about objects in the classroom.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in interactive games by listening to commands.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks: "Did you understand 'Please, stand up'?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks: "Did you hear 'book' today?" (Students point to a book if they heard and understood it).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite action to do today?" (Students can show the action or point to a flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you hear today?" (Teacher points to different flashcards, students nod when they hear one they remember).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? The TPR activities and "Teacher Says" game were highly engaging, and most students responded well to simple, visual instructions. Modeling pronunciation of /s/ and /l/ was effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges arose? Some students struggled with multi-step instructions (e.g., "Stand up and open your book") and required more repetition or visual cues. Distinguishing between 'teacher' and 'student' syllable stress needed more practice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments/scaffolds are needed for next time? Incorporate more repetitive practice for compound instructions. Use clearer, larger visuals for classroom objects. Prepare a few extra physical activities for quick energy release.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple illustrated text that uses the same vocabulary and imperative commands introduced in this listening lesson. The goal will be to recognize the words in written form and connect them to their meaning through visual context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>